From 045bb07a5463209a77a010bf1e17e673b79e5774 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 19:14:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] make the write path validate what the read path rejects
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A defect I introduced. When shared/ingest.ts started validating every row
against ProjectSchema on load, the write path was left doing
`(project as any)[field] = value` with no check at all. The two halves
disagreed, and the disagreement destroyed data:

  1. a user types a comment longer than ProjectSchema's 5,000-character limit
  2. update() writes it to localStorage without complaint
  3. on the next load ingestProjects rejects that row
  4. the cache is judged untrustworthy, discarded, and refetched
  5. every *other* local edit is replaced by the shipped snapshot

One over-long comment silently lost the lot. Reproduced before fixing:
1 accepted, 1 rejected, `clean: false` -> whole cache discarded.

Both halves fixed:
- projects.update() and reviews.update() validate the candidate row with the
  same schema the read path uses, and throw a German message naming the field
  and the reason. The old value stays.
- A cache with one bad row now keeps its good rows and rewrites itself without
  them, instead of refetching. Refetching replaced every local edit to punish
  a single row, which is a far larger loss than the row. Writes are validated
  now, so a bad row here means corruption from outside the app.

Two more holes on the same path:
- applyEdit and applyReviewEdit passed `field as any`, discarding the one
  guarantee ProjectUpdateInput offered — a mistyped field name compiled
  cleanly and surfaced only as a wrong write at runtime. Both are typed
  against the input now.
- A refused write was silent: the optimistic value rolled back and nothing
  told the user why, which reads as the app eating their typing. Both
  mutations now surface the reason.

shared/write-read-symmetry.test.ts pins it: what the schema rejects is
rejected by ingest, what it accepts is accepted, every field the table can
edit has a schema rule, and a three-row cache with one bad row keeps rows
1 and 3.

All five `as any` are gone from the write path; lint 85 -> 80.

Gates: 0 type errors, 129 passed / 6 skipped, 80 lint warnings, build clean,
e2e 20 passed / 0 failed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
